---
name: my-skill
description: One paragraph, written for an agent deciding whether to load this. Say what it does, what it produces, and — most importantly — the situations and phrasings that should trigger it ("Use when the user asks to …", "or mentions X, Y, Z"). This is the only text a consumer sees before loading the skill, so a vague description means the skill never fires.
---

# my-skill

What this skill is for, in a sentence or two. Assume the reader is an agent
that has just loaded it mid-task, not a human browsing a catalog.

## When to use it

The concrete triggers, and the cases where something else is the better tool.

## How to use it

The actual procedure. Prefer numbered steps with the exact commands.

1. …
2. …

## Files

- `scripts/…` — executables. Keep the git executable bit on real scripts and
  off everything else; an executable file the scanner cannot read (a `.xsd`, a
  `.tmpl`) is a finding, and at community trust a finding blocks the install.
- `references/…` — longer docs the skill reads on demand rather than inlining.
- `templates/…` — files the skill copies out.
- `assets/…` — images, fonts, binaries it ships.

## Before you push

Run `node tools/validate.mjs my-skill`. It applies the same rules the skills
service applies on install: path and size caps, and the content scanner at
trust `community`, where any finding at all is fatal. See the README for the
prose patterns that trip it.

